    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4c07e9cf_25541a39",
        "filename": "zephyr/emul/tcpc/emul_tcpci_partner_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 762,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-22T22:14:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was waiting to get your feedback on this approach. I had intended this to be used as a means to test for relevant parts of the Enter_USB data object contents. My thought was that the test setup could set the field(s) that needed to be checked against. If the field was left as 0, then it would be a don\u0027t care. Extending this idea, for now, I was just using it as a simply binary with 0 (default) meaning accept, and non-zero triggers a reject.\n\nBut, I since realized that the message checking could be done in the test source file via the PD message log and parsing the message contents from there. In that case, we\u0027d still want some bool here to control ACCEPT/REJECT from the partner port.",
      "parentUuid": "a1cd77e6_8ddcc5ed",
      "revId": "b9bb3284e073585d1ce07d994259c98f0d14da55",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "442b5087_c45504e0",
        "filename": "zephyr/emul/tcpc/emul_tcpci_partner_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 762,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-23T19:13:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To be consistent with our approach so far, you could just add a `bool` to the partner state that says whether to Accept or Reject any Enter USB and set that field from the test setup. We\u0027ve done very little message validation on the partner side so far.\n\nIf you wanted to validate the EUDO in detail, you could do it from the message log in the test.\n\nIf you wanted to validate messages on the partner side, I\u0027d just store an EUDO in the partner state during test setup and then compare to it exactly. I wouldn\u0027t complicate it by trying to put wildcards in EUDO fields. If we want to do something complicated like that, we could encode it in test logic.\n\nOne thing you could potentially do is only validate the fields that should be obvious from the discovery info provided by the partner/cable. Looking through the PD description of _Enter_USB_, I think that all of the fields can either be derived from the discovery data or should currently be the same for all USB4 CrOS devices. That would avoid storing redundant or potentially inconsistent data in the partner.\n\nI think, for the purpose of this first set of tests, what I\u0027d most prefer would be to do zero validation on the partner side and just configure Accept/Reject. My second choice would be to validate the EUDO based on the discovery data that\u0027s already there.",
      "parentUuid": "4c07e9cf_25541a39",
      "revId": "b9bb3284e073585d1ce07d994259c98f0d14da55",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d7751246_141cc623",
        "filename": "zephyr/emul/tcpc/emul_tcpci_partner_common.c",
